    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper presents results of an analogue field study of fractures and faults in the Devonian tight sandstones of the Huamampampa and Santa Rosa formations in the Sub-Andean fold and thrust belt, 30 km west of Tarija, SE Bolivia. The faults and fractures have been classified into three major groups:\r\n\r\n1) Joints which are commonly strata bound,\r\n2).faults formed by sheared joint mechanism and their heights scale by the thicknesses of sequences and parasequences , and\r\n3) faults localized regionally in shale horizons involving megasequences.\r\n\r\nJoints in low strain domains demonstrate the characteristics of the early joints systems: two orthogonal sets parallel and perpendicular to the tectonic transport direction and are about perpendicular to bedding. The spacing of these joints is variable and controlled primarily by bed thickness and sequence stratigraphic characteristics of the sandstone formations.\r\n\r\nFaults formed by shearing of early bedding interfaces, joints, and splay joints of several generations. Bed-parallel faults are of thrust type. Shearing of early joints and their splay joints are of strike-slip, normal, and sometimes oblique (strike- slip and normal) types. Thrust faults involving thick shale units are the dominant features at regional scale.\r\n\r\nThe spacing of joints and small faults presents a lognormal distribution, whereas the intermediate and large fault zones show a normal distribution. The main factors controlling azimuth-parallel fracture intensity and distribution are the degree of shear along faults perpendicular to the fold axis and the proximity to major thrust faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
